{
 "id": "2020-dicristofaro-trees",
 "name": "Trees Outside Forests Landscape Ranking",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the visual choice experiment of di Cristofaro, Sallustio, Sitzia, Marchetti & Lasserre (2020). How do trees outside forests (TOF: single trees, rows and small woods in towns and farmland) change landscape preference along an urban-rural-natural gradient? The authors photographed six landscapes of the Molise region in Italy (homogeneous and heterogeneous urban, rural and forest landscapes) and edited each with a raster graphics editor into three versions: no TOF, medium TOF cover (1.7% of the viewshed, the regional average) and high TOF cover (3.4%). Participants first answer the paper's personal questions (gender, age group, inhabitant or visitor, home in town or out of town, profession, education, marital status). They then rank the three versions of each landscape, and finally rank the six medium-cover landscapes. Analysis as in the paper: preference distributions per TOF level and landscape, an overall score per photo (rank within the triplet times the landscape's rank), chi-square tests between respondent groups, and relations with landscape metrics. Of 360 respondents intercepted in Molise, high TOF cover was most preferred in the urban landscapes (86.9% and 72.8%), and photos without TOF were least preferred in urban and rural landscapes. In the natural landscapes, high TOF cover was least preferred, and the homogeneous forest landscape was most liked without TOF (62.5%). Source: di Cristofaro, M., Sallustio, L., Sitzia, T., Marchetti, M. & Lasserre, B. (2020), Landscape Preference for Trees Outside Forests along an Urban-Rural-Natural Gradient, Forests 11(7): 728, doi:10.3390/f11070728, CC BY 4.0. Photos are cropped from its Figure 2. The template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper showed each triplet on A2 sheets in face-to-face interviews in public places of Molise, with quotas by age, gender, origin and residence; here the photos are shown on screen and anyone can take part; (2) the paper does not say how the photos were placed on the sheets, so each triplet is shown in the printed order (no, medium, high TOF) and the final six are shown in order L1-L6; (3) participants tap the photos from most to least preferred instead of writing ranks 1-3 (least to most), and ranks are stored from most to least preferred; (4) the paper names the personal questions but not their wording or answer options, which are ours; (5) the photos are the copies printed in Figure 2, about 670 x 360 px.",
