  "description": "Faithful replication of the visual choice experiment of di Cristofaro, Sallustio, Sitzia, Marchetti & Lasserre (2020), Forests 11(7): 728. Six landscapes of the Molise region, Italy, along an urban-rural-natural gradient (L1 homogeneous urban, L2 heterogeneous urban, L3 homogeneous rural, L4 heterogeneous rural, L5 heterogeneous forest, L6 homogeneous forest) were photographed by the authors and edited with a raster graphics editor into three versions each: no trees outside forests (TOF), medium TOF cover (1.7% of the viewshed) and high TOF cover (3.4%). Participants rank the three versions of each landscape by preference, then rank the six medium-cover photos. Rankings are stored as arrays from most to least preferred. Analysis as in the paper: preference distributions (least, intermediate, most preferred) for each TOF level within each landscape, an overall score per photo (rank within the triplet times the rank of the landscape in the final sequence), chi-square tests between respondent groups (age, gender, inhabitants vs. visitors, in town vs. out of town), and relations between preference and landscape metrics. With 360 respondents intercepted in Molise, high TOF cover was most preferred in the urban landscapes (86.9% in L1, 72.8% in L2) and photos without TOF were least preferred in urban and rural landscapes, while in the natural landscapes high TOF cover was least preferred and the homogeneous forest landscape was most liked without TOF (62.5%). Differences from the original study: (1) the paper showed each triplet on A2 sheets in face-to-face interviews in public places of Molise, with quotas by age, gender, origin and residence; here the photos are shown on screen and anyone can take part; (2) the paper does not say how the photos were placed on the sheets, so each triplet is shown in the printed order (no, medium, high TOF) and the final six in order L1-L6; (3) participants tap photos from most to least preferred instead of writing 1-3 (least to most), and the ranks are stored from most to least preferred; (4) the paper names the personal questions (profession, education, marital status, age group, gender, inhabitant or visitor, home in town or out of town) but not their wording or answer options, which are ours; (5) the photos are the copies printed in Figure 2 (about 670 x 360 px).",
